       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Your confirmation doesn't change the dose, and I can't verify the clinical rationale from here.\n\nIbuprofen 1600 mg four times daily is 6,400 mg/day. That's double the maximum labeled daily dose, and no standard indication calls for it. Each 1,600 mg dose is also double the maximum single dose. At this level the risks of GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events go up, and there's no analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit beyond the ceiling dose. A mistake here could seriously harm the patient.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a dose within the maximum.** I can enter 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day) or any lower regimen right now.\n- **Use your hospital's process for non-standard doses.** Have the attending or a pharmacist review and document the rationale. They may be able to place the order with an override, or the order may need pharmacy verification before it's released.\n- **Look at alternatives.** If the aim is stronger anti-inflammatory or analgesic effect, pharmacy can suggest other agents or a multimodal regimen.\n\nIf you tell me the dose and frequency you'd like within the usual limits, I'll enter it."
